.TH SESSION 2
.SH NAME
session \- what a person did to a session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "session.m";
session := load Session Session->PATH;

Atype, Akey, Aclick, Adouble, Adrag, Amove, Await, Aresize, Apause: con iota;

Action: adt {
    kind:   int;
    at:     int;
    text:   string;
    x, y:   int;
    x2, y2: int;
    b:      int;

    script: fn(a: self ref Action): string;
    play:   fn(a: self ref Action): string;
};

init:  fn(): string;
read:  fn(path: string): (array of ref Action, string);
parse: fn(words: list of string): (ref Action, string);
span:  fn(a: array of ref Action): int;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
A recording made by
.B INFERNO_INPUT_RECORD
(see
.IR emu (1))
is key presses and releases and pointer states.
It reproduces a session exactly and reads like nothing at all.
.B Session
turns it into
.IR actions :
typed this, clicked there, waited.
.PP
.B Read
decodes a recording.
.B Script
renders one action as a line of shell, and
.B parse
reads such a line back, so a script edited by hand is understood by the same
code that wrote it.
.B Play
performs an action.
.B Span
is how long the whole recording lasts, in milliseconds.
.SS "What the decoding does"
Releases are dropped.
Runs of printable keys become one
.B Atype
with the text.
A press and release of a button at one place is a click; two of those within
500ms is a double click; one with movement between is a drag.
Movement with no button held is reported only where it stops.
Some of it is kept rather than none because a widget that tracks the pointer,
such as a menu, would otherwise never see it move \(em though no script here
has yet driven a menu, so that is the reason for keeping it rather than a
demonstration that this much is enough.
Quiet longer than 250ms becomes an explicit wait.
.SS "How an action is performed"
Through the two files the system already has for it:
.B /dev/keyboard
which has always accepted written keys, and
.B /dev/pointerin
(see
.IR pointer (3)).
Both are below every bind, so this drives
.IR wm (1)
and its clients and anything they started.
.PP
A click is written with explicit times rather than left to the clock, because a
consumer decides a double click from the difference between the times the
events carry.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/session.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh-session (1),
.IR emu (1),
.IR pointer (3),
.IR cons (3)
.SH BUGS
A session driven by a script cannot be recorded.
.B /dev/pointerin
and writes to
.B /dev/keyboard
deliberately do not record \(em on a replay the program that wrote them runs
again and writes them again, so recording them would double everything \(em
which means the machine cannot record its own injections.
So there is no record, edit, record again: a recording is made by a person at
the keyboard, and everything after that is a script.
.PP
.B Aresize
decodes but cannot be performed: the host owns the window size.
A recording that resized the window replays into whatever size the window
already has, and since pointer coordinates are absolute, the clicks then land
somewhere else.
.PP
A key release cannot be told from the German dead keys
.RB ( Grave ,
.BR Acute ,
.BR Circumflex ).
.B Keyup
is
.B Spec|0x800
and German is
.BR Spec|0xf00 ,
so the ranges overlap.
That is in the encoding rather than here, and it means a German keyboard's dead
keys decode as releases and vanish.
.PP
It reads what arrived, not what was meant.
A key held down long enough to repeat is several presses and decodes as
several characters, which is what the system saw.
